# A homestead on an island, in the rain. The standing shot.
#
# **This scene is a changelog, not a unit test, and that is the whole reason it
# exists.** Every other file in this directory isolates one system so that a
# regression in it shows up in exactly one reference and nowhere else. This one
# runs most of them at once — terrain, water, current, ocean, grass, rain,
# wetness, shelter, both particle blend modes, wind, an authored environment —
# from a camera that is never going to move again. So it will move its golden
# image whenever *anything* changes, and it is supposed to.
#
# **Do not "fix" that churn by taking it out of `GOLDEN`.** The value is not
# that the reference stays still; it is that every time it moves, the move is a
# readable line in `tests/references/MANIFEST.txt` and a visible diff of a whole
# frame. Nothing else in this repo shows what a change did to a *picture* rather
# than to one system. Re-bless it deliberately, look at the diff, and if you
# cannot explain what moved, that is the bug.
#
# Its own gate value is real but different from the others': it is the only
# scene where two systems can interfere. A wetness term that reads the grass
# blade's normal, a water depth grid that the rain shelter march also wants, a
# particle sort that the sky pass invalidates — none of those are visible in a
# scene that exercises one path at a time.
#
#     loom render assets/test/homestead.loom --sim 1800 --out shot.png \
#       --size 1280x800
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What each element is for, and what breaking it looks like *here*
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **The island — voxel CSG** (`Ground`). One volume, eleven ops, in the order
# they are written: sea floor, dome, channel, a rock scoop, then the house.
# ORDER MATTERS everywhere in this file; the channel is subtracted before the
# house is added, which is what lets the terrace sit on the bank rather than be
# cut away by it. *Broken:* the shoreline stops matching the water's edge, or
# the channel becomes a tunnel — a straight capsule chord under a convex dome
# dips below the surface in the middle even when it clears at both ends, and
# then the creek is a cave with a lake at each end. As authored, the chord's
# worst clearance is at z = 17.4 and any radius over 1.61 m breaks the surface
# there; 4.2 m is chosen for the channel's *width*, not for that margin. Move
# either shape and check the middle, not the ends.
#
# **48 m across at 0.375 m voxels — deliberately coarser than `shore.loom`'s
# 0.25.** Bake cost is per *chunk*, not per metre: [4,2,4] is 32 chunks against
# `shore`'s 72 at the same footprint, and dropping to it cut a render by about
# 45%. Measured on the debug build the gates use: this scene 4.8 s, `shore`
# 3.6 s, `grass_slope` 2.7 s, `terrain_stress` 15.4 s — so a scene carrying
# several systems at once costs a third more than one carrying a slice of it,
# and a quarter of what the stress scene costs. **Trim the resolution before
# the content** if it ever needs to come down again; the content is the point.
#
# The house's walls and roof are 0.8 m — a hair over two voxels — for the reason
# `rain_overhang.loom` gives: S3 seals a one-voxel roof and that is already
# tested, so nothing here should be sitting on the limit. The shelter
# assertions below are what would go quietly soft if it were.
#
# **The sea and the river are ONE `WaterBody`, and that is the interesting
# part.** `World::water()` takes the first and there is deliberately no way to
# ask for a second: a `WaterBody` has no extent in the schema, so two would be
# two infinite planes with nothing to say where one stops. Since ADR 0013 the
# water mesh reaches the true horizon, so one sea-level body plus a `flow`
# field gives *both* — a current where the island's drainage channels it,
# open ocean everywhere the terrain grid does not reach. Nothing here says
# where the river runs; `loom_water::flow` routes water downhill across the
# same height grid the depth comes from and takes the direction from the
# neighbourhood's discharge.
#
#     loom water assets/test/homestead.loom --at 24,25   # -0.93 m/s down -Z
#     loom water assets/test/homestead.loom --at 24,14   # -1.28 m/s, faster
#     loom water assets/test/homestead.loom --at 24,-30  # 0, and bed: null
#
# The third is the proof that one body is doing two jobs: no bed, no current,
# open sea. *Broken:* the current reverses, or wanders off the channel onto the
# banks, or the ocean acquires a drift — all three are `flow` reading the wrong
# gradient, and the middle one is the bug the smoothing radius exists to stop.
#
# **Grass — three fields, not one, and the reason is water.** `loom_grass` has
# no idea where the sea is: coverage is slope, rock and gully-flow, and a
# gully is *lusher*, so a single rectangle over this island would grow a lawn
# down the middle of the river bed and out across the sea floor. Three
# rectangles that each stay above the water line is the honest fix, and they
# carry `slope_cutoff = 0.45` so the blades run down the banks instead of
# stopping in a shaved line at the rim. *Broken:* blades underwater, a hard
# straight edge across a bank, or the field floating above / sunk into the
# terrain — the last is the SDF height march, which is the expensive part of
# loading this scene.
#
# **The house — voxel CSG, because shelter is not a mesh property.** Rain
# exposure is `loom_voxel::exposure` marching the SDF; a `MeshRenderer` box
# would keep no rain off at all. The roof is what makes these hold:
#
#     loom sim assets/test/homestead.loom --ticks 1 \
#       --assert "rain@31.7,10.5,15.7.rate < 0.05" \
#       --assert "rain@17,10.5,24.rate > 11.9" \
#       --assert "rain@24,12,30.rate > 11.9"
#
# Inside the shack: nothing. On the grass and over the creek: the full 12 mm/h.
# *Broken:* the interior reading climbs — a thinned roof, an exposure march
# that stopped honouring the node's translation, or a shelter query that reads
# the camera's exposure instead of the sample point's.
#
# **Rain, wetness, and why the banks shine.** At `--sim 1800` the film is at its
# ceiling and the soak is about half way — measured, and asserted:
#
#     loom sim assets/test/homestead.loom --ticks 1800 \
#       --assert "wetness@17,10.5,24.film > 0.99" \
#       --assert "wetness@17,10.5,24.soak > 0.4" \
#       --assert "wetness@17,10.5,24.soak < 0.7" \
#       --assert "wetness@31.7,10.5,15.7.film < 0.01"
#
# The last line is the one that matters: the floor inside the door is bone dry
# thirty seconds into a downpour, because the same voxel roof that stops the
# rain stops the wetness. Everything exposed is glossy and darkened; every
# sheltered surface is matt. The wet inner face of the east bank is the
# brightest non-sky thing in the frame: a smooth SDF surface with a water film
# reflecting a bright horizon at a grazing angle. It is physically what the
# model says and it is on the edge of blowing out; if wetness gains a
# roughness floor, this is the pixel that will move first. *Broken:* the whole
# island goes matt (wetness detached from the rain rate), or the sheltered
# surfaces shine too (exposure not reaching the material).
#
# **Fire and smoke — the two blend modes, side by side.** The fire is
# `additive`, because overlapping alpha flames darken each other into grey mud
# while additive ones brighten where they pile up; its alpha is low for the
# same reason `explosion.loom` gives, so only the core blows out. The smoke
# above it is ordinary sorted alpha, because smoke occludes. They share a spot
# in the doorway, which is the frame's only warm light and the thing that tells
# you the dark box is a house. *Broken:* the smoke stops sorting and flickers
# against the roof, or the fire turns into a flat white dome (additive alpha
# too high), or the plume stops leaning (see wind).
#
# **Wind drives three things here and they must agree.** The grass bends, the
# smoke leans (`wind_response = 4.0`, matching the air in a quarter second) and
# the rain streaks tilt — all from one `loom_field::wind` sample. *Broken:* they
# disagree. Blades leaning one way and smoke the other is the CPU and GPU
# evaluating different formulas, which is exactly what S2's generator exists to
# make impossible; if you ever see it, the generated Slang was hand-edited.
#
# **Environment.** Low sun over the sea, off to the left of the frame so the
# glitter path lands inside it and the rain is lit from three-quarters behind.
# Fog separates the island from the horizon rather than hiding anything. The
# camera sits seven metres above the water and looks down the channel: Fresnel
# on water needs a grazing angle, and this is where the surface goes from
# "seeing into it" in the creek to "seeing the sky in it" at the horizon, in one
# shot.
# *Broken:* the sea flattens to one colour (Fresnel or the depth tint), or the
# horizon hardens into an edge (the water mesh stopped reaching it).
#
# **`--yaw`/`--pitch` is not useful on this scene, and that is worth knowing
# before you try it.** Overriding the camera drops back to auto-framing from
# *mesh* bounds, and the only meshes here are a fire pit and a post — so the
# orbit frames those two and puts the eye under water. `cargo xtask flythrough`
# is the second angle that works: it keeps the authored camera and spins it,
# which is what the sixteen frames of grass, waves and rain in motion need
# anyway.
#
# **What is deliberately NOT here.** Nothing floats. `river.loom` proves a
# current by carrying a crate downstream, but this scene's golden frame is at
# tick 1800 — thirty seconds — and anything the creek picked up would be out at
# sea and out of frame, proving the current only by its absence. So the current
# is asserted with `loom water` instead. The post in the creek is there to give
# the surface a fixed silhouette to move against, which is what
# `cargo xtask flythrough` needs and a still cannot show.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "e3d7b1a5-84c2-4f60-9b13-5a2e7c064d98"

[[node]]
name = "Homestead"

  # Low sun out over the sea, 14 degrees to the left of the camera's axis and
  # 13 degrees above the horizon. Both numbers were chosen against renders
  # rather than from taste: further left and the glitter path leaves the frame,
  # much higher and the water goes matt because Fresnel needs the grazing
  # angle. Everything camera-facing is therefore three-quarter backlit and the
  # house reads as a silhouette with a fire in the door — that is the intent,
  # not an unlit material.
  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [-0.14, 0.23, -0.96]
  sun_strength = 1.3
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.80, 0.56]
  ambient = 0.50
  sky_zenith = [0.07, 0.09, 0.15]
  sky_horizon = [0.27, 0.24, 0.23]
  fog_density = 0.006
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # Blowing across the frame, toward -X and slightly toward the camera.
  # **The one field three systems read**: grass bend, smoke advection
  # (`wind_response` below) and the tilt of the rain streaks. If they ever
  # disagree, the CPU and GPU are evaluating different formulas — see the
  # header.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 165.0
  speed = 8.0
  gustiness = 1.5
  turbulence = 1.2
  ground_drag = 0.45

  # Heavy, and it never stops — there is no `duration`, because this scene is
  # a standing shot rather than a dry-down test (`rain_overhang.loom` owns
  # that). 12 mm/h is well past `loom_rain::SOAKING_RATE` (2.0), so the
  # wetness equilibrium is 1.0 and every exposed surface is as wet as it can
  # get; the rate is chosen for how many streaks are drawn, which is the other
  # question the same unit answers.
  [node.components.Rain]
  intensity = 12.0

[[node]]
name = "Ground"
parent = "Homestead"

  [node.components.VoxelVolume]
  voxel_size = 0.375
  chunks = [4, 2, 4]

  # The sea floor, top at y = 2 — six metres under the still-water line, which
  # is deeper than anything that reads depth. `shore.loom` states that rule and
  # the reason: the shoaling taper saturates at `attenuation_depth` (4 m) and
  # the shallow tint at `WATER_TINT_DEPTH` (5 m), so a shallower floor draws the
  # grid's own boundary as a hard rectangle in the sea. It runs past the
  # volume's edge on purpose — beyond it there is no terrain, depth is the
  # sentinel, and the water draws itself as bottomless open ocean.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [24.0, 1.0, 24.0]
  half_extents = [26.0, 1.0, 26.0]
  mode = "union"

  # The island. A sphere, because its flank passes through every angle, so the
  # water line and the grass's slope cutoff both land where the terrain puts
  # them rather than on a seam somebody authored. Centred 18.7 m down with
  # radius 29.7: summit y = 11, water line at r = 13 (a 26 m island), base at
  # r = 21.3 — which is what has to fit inside a 48 m volume with a margin, and
  # is the constraint that fixes every other number in this file.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "sphere"
  center = [24.0, -18.7, 24.0]
  radius = 29.7
  mode = "union"

  # The channel, cut from the summit out to sea along -Z. Its bed descends from
  # y = 6.4 to y = 1.4, and THAT descent is the entire reason there is a
  # current: flow direction is the bed's gradient and a flat bed has none.
  #
  # **A straight chord under a convex dome is the trap.** The dome falls away
  # faster than the axis does, so a radius that clears the surface at both ends
  # can still pass *below* it in the middle — and then the cut is a tunnel, not
  # a channel. Here the worst clearance is at z = 17.4, where the ground is
  # 1.61 m above the axis, so anything over that radius surfaces along the whole
  # run. 4.2 m is far past it and is chosen for width: a narrower cut gave a
  # six-metre slot with vertical walls that filled the frame, and widening it
  # is what let the grass reach down the banks.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "capsule"
  a = [24.0, 10.6, 26.0]
  b = [24.0, 5.6, 4.0]
  radius = 4.2
  mode = "subtract"

  # One scoop out of the west bank, so the rim is not a smooth machined arc for
  # its whole length. Bigger rocks were tried as `union` spheres and read as
  # wet black domes: a sphere under a water film reflects the sky, and at this
  # albedo that is the shiniest thing in the frame. Subtracting is safe;
  # adding is not.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "sphere"
  center = [19.6, 10.6, 24.5]
  radius = 2.6
  mode = "subtract"

  # --- the house, on the +X bank -------------------------------------------

  # The terrace, top at y = 9.9. **Deep (bottom y = 3.5) and only just proud of
  # the ground**: the depth is so it stays grounded where it overhangs the
  # channel, and the 9.9 is because at 10.5 its retaining face stood a metre
  # clear of the hill and read as a sheet of wet white card in the middle of
  # the shot. Lower it and it buries; raise it and the face comes back.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [32.0, 6.7, 16.5]
  half_extents = [3.0, 3.2, 3.5]
  mode = "union"

  # Walls and roof, 0.8 m — a hair over two voxels at 0.375 m. **Two, not one,
  # for the reason `rain_overhang.loom` gives**: S3 seals a one-voxel roof and
  # that is already tested, so nothing here should sit on the limit. The shelter
  # assertion at the top of this file is what would go quietly soft if it did.
  # Wall height 2.8 m, floor 9.9 to eaves 12.7.
  #
  # Back wall (-Z).
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [31.75, 11.3, 13.9]
  half_extents = [2.75, 1.4, 0.4]
  mode = "union"

  # Left wall (-X).
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [29.4, 11.3, 15.75]
  half_extents = [0.4, 1.4, 2.0]
  mode = "union"

  # Right wall (+X).
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [34.1, 11.3, 15.75]
  half_extents = [0.4, 1.4, 2.0]
  mode = "union"

  # Front wall (+Z), facing the camera.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [31.75, 11.3, 17.35]
  half_extents = [2.75, 1.4, 0.4]
  mode = "union"

  # Roof.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [31.75, 13.05, 16.0]
  half_extents = [3.4, 0.4, 2.9]
  mode = "union"

  # The doorway, punched through the front wall — 1.4 m wide, 1.8 m tall, and
  # the only opening. It faces the camera so the fire outside it is the frame's
  # one warm light. The interior behind it is where the `rain@...rate < 0.05`
  # assertion is taken.
  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [31.2, 10.8, 17.35]
  half_extents = [0.7, 0.9, 0.7]
  mode = "subtract"

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.15, 0.145, 0.105]
  roughness = 0.95

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Homestead"

  # **The scene's only water body, and it is both the river and the ocean.**
  # See the header. `kind` is a label — nothing in the renderer or the physics
  # branches on it; the only code that reads it is `loom water`, which echoes
  # it back. This one is a river that happens to reach the sea.
  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "river"
  surface_height = 8.0
  density = 1025.0
  drag = 60.0

  # The only thing authored about the current. Where it goes is the ground's
  # business. Set this to 0.0 and the two `loom water --at` readings in the
  # header collapse to zero while the ocean is unchanged, which is the cheapest
  # falsification of the claim that one body is doing two jobs.
  [node.components.WaterBody.flow]
  speed = 2.5

  # Swell running onshore, toward +Z. Four waves with no common factor, longest
  # first, so the sum does not repeat on a visible grid; nothing shorter than
  # 4.7 m because the LOD rings stop resolving it (ADR 0012, and `ocean.loom`
  # explains the number). `attenuation_depth = 4` is shallower than the 6 m sea
  # floor, so the swell arrives at the beach flattened and the creek — 2 to 5 m
  # deep — is nearly still while the open sea is not.
  [node.components.WaterBody.waves]
  attenuation_depth = 4.0
  max_height = 1.3

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 22.0
  amplitude = 0.62
  steepness = 0.35
  direction = [0.15, 1.0]
  speed_scale = 1.0

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 13.0
  amplitude = 0.34
  steepness = 0.45
  direction = [-0.25, 1.0]
  speed_scale = 1.0

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 8.0
  amplitude = 0.19
  steepness = 0.5
  direction = [0.45, 1.0]
  speed_scale = 1.0

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 4.7
  amplitude = 0.09
  steepness = 0.55
  direction = [-0.15, 1.0]
  speed_scale = 1.0

# Three fields, each a rectangle that stays above the water line and off the
# river. `loom_grass` does not know where the sea is — see the header — so this
# is the only thing keeping blades out of it. **Check the corners** if you move
# one: the island's water line is a circle of radius 13 about [24, ., 24], and
# a rectangle's corner is the bit that escapes it.
#
# `slope_cutoff = 0.45` rather than the 0.7 default so the blades run down the
# banks. At 0.7 they stopped in a shaved line at the rim and the wet bank below
# read as poured concrete.

[[node]]
name = "GrassWest"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [16.75, 0.0, 23.0] }

  [node.components.Grass]
  half_extent = [3.4, 6.0]
  slope_cutoff = 0.45
  density = 150.0
  height = 0.42
  width = 0.020
  clump_facing = 0.75
  clump_colour = 0.4

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.27, 0.40, 0.13]
  roughness = 0.75

[[node]]
name = "GrassEast"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [30.75, 0.0, 25.5] }

  [node.components.Grass]
  half_extent = [4.25, 4.5]
  slope_cutoff = 0.45
  density = 150.0
  height = 0.42
  width = 0.020
  clump_facing = 0.75
  clump_colour = 0.4

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.27, 0.40, 0.13]
  roughness = 0.75

[[node]]
name = "GrassNear"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [26.0, 0.0, 32.15] }

  [node.components.Grass]
  half_extent = [4.5, 2.35]
  slope_cutoff = 0.45
  density = 150.0
  height = 0.42
  width = 0.020
  clump_facing = 0.75
  clump_colour = 0.4

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.27, 0.40, 0.13]
  roughness = 0.75

# The fire pit, on the terrace a metre outside the door. **Outside on purpose**:
# under the eaves the plume would climb straight into the roof, and particles do
# not collide with terrain.

[[node]]
name = "FirePit"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [31.2, 9.95, 18.8], scale = [0.55, 0.14, 0.55] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "cylinder" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.10, 0.08, 0.07]
  roughness = 0.9

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADDITIVE. Overlapping alpha-blended flames darken each other into grey-brown
# mud; additive ones brighten where they pile up, which is what fire does. The
# alpha is low for the reason `explosion.loom` gives — additive light
# accumulates, and at 0.9 a hundred sprites saturate into a flat white dome
# that reads as a lamp.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[[node]]
name = "Fire"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [31.2, 10.1, 18.8] }

  [node.components.ParticleEmitter]
  rate = 170.0
  lifetime = 0.6
  lifetime_jitter = 0.4
  speed = 2.4
  spread_degrees = 22.0
  radius = 0.18
  gravity = 3.2
  drag = 2.2
  turbulence = 2.6
  turbulence_scale = 0.8
  wind_response = 1.2
  size = [0.40, 1.05]
  color_start = [1.0, 0.80, 0.36]
  color_end = [0.72, 0.16, 0.03]
  alpha = [0.3, 0.0]
  additive = true
  seed = 5501

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALPHA, sorted, because smoke occludes. `wind_response = 4.0` matches the air
# in about a quarter of a second, which is what smoke does; the fire above is
# 1.2 because a flame is anchored to its fuel and only leans. Short-lived on
# purpose: at `lifetime = 3.4` the plume crossed the whole frame as a pale band
# and washed out the sea behind it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[[node]]
name = "Smoke"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [31.2, 10.8, 18.8] }

  [node.components.ParticleEmitter]
  rate = 40.0
  lifetime = 2.0
  lifetime_jitter = 0.4
  speed = 1.5
  spread_degrees = 24.0
  radius = 0.28
  gravity = 1.5
  drag = 0.9
  turbulence = 1.6
  turbulence_scale = 0.3
  wind_response = 4.0
  size = [0.45, 2.4]
  color_start = [0.16, 0.14, 0.13]
  color_end = [0.42, 0.42, 0.45]
  alpha = [0.38, 0.0]
  seed = 8837

# The fire's light. Additive particles are not a light source — they brighten
# their own pixels and nothing else — so without this the doorway and the wall
# beside it stay as dark as the rest of the backlit house. 90 rather than the
# 300 `explosion.loom` uses: this one is two metres from a wall, not seven.

[[node]]
name = "Hearth"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [31.2, 10.6, 18.8] }

  [node.components.Light]
  intensity = 90.0
  color = [1.0, 0.55, 0.20]

# A post standing in the creek. Its whole job is to have a known silhouette for
# the water to move against: a still frame cannot tell a current from a pond,
# and `cargo xtask flythrough` is where that shows. Its base is 2.2 m under the
# surface, so it looks like it starts at the water line — that is water, not a
# floating box. It is driven 0.4 m into the bed rather than resting on it,
# because the bed here is a voxel surface and a change of resolution moves it.

[[node]]
name = "MooringPost"
parent = "Homestead"
transform = { pos = [23.0, 7.7, 25.0], scale = [0.16, 1.9, 0.16] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.16, 0.13, 0.10]
  roughness = 0.85

# **The shot. Do not move it** — the whole point of this file is that the same
# frame is re-rendered as the engine changes, so a camera tweak throws away
# every comparison against every earlier reference.
#
# Seven metres above the back of the island, looking down the channel: the
# creek runs away from the eye into the sea, the house sits on the right bank
# with its fire, the grass is foreground and mid-ground, and the horizon is the
# open ocean the same water body draws. Earlier cameras sat lower and closer to
# the rim and the two banks filled the frame as black and white wedges; this
